llywood celebrity  for those of you who didn't notice she's  also descended from men  elves and the angelic mayar giving her  and her immediate family a unique  connection to all three races  according to the appendix of the return  of the king arwen is born 241 years into  the third age  that makes her approximately 2778 years  old by the time the one ring is  destroyed  which isn't terribly old for an elf in  comparison  galadriel is over 8 000 however it does  give her a good step up on her future  husband's 88-year lifespan  when arwen is born the world is  relatively peaceful a few hundred years  away from the epic war of the last  alliance  that's the event depicted in the opening  sequence of the fellowship of the ring  where men elves and dwarves join  together to resist sauron's first run at  conquering the continent  when the dust settles the dark lord is  defeated the ring has been cut from his  hand  and middle-earth settles down for a long  period of relative peace and prosperity  the primary thing to point out during  these early centuries of armin's life is  the fact that while the power of men  grows in middle earth  the elves and dwarves slowly begin to  fade into obscurity  and that's not to say that they're  inconsequential places like lothlorien  rivendell and khazad doom are still  powerful and influential  however they stop trending up at this  point in time and if anything  begin to lose the larger political and  military power that they once had  her people may have been slowly fading  but the first few thousand years of  arwen's life are ones of peaceful  comfort  she spends a lot of her time bouncing  between rivendell and lothlorian with  her grandparents  in the return of the king she even  casually mentions that she'd been in  lothlorian for a while  while there aren't a ton of details on  arwen's early activity the bulk of her  time for the greater part of 3000 years  seems to have been spent following this  pattern of living in one of the two  elven havens  or traveling between them in addition to  her famous father and grandmother arwen  also has two older twin brothers  ella dan and elro here as well as a  mother named calebrion  however over time she sees each of these  familial connections less often  primarily due to a tragic event that  happens to her mother about 500 years  before the war of the ring  tragedy strikes the family when their  mother is captured by orcs in the misty  mountains  she's eventually found and rescued by  her sons but by then  she's been tortured and poisoned a  devastated elrond manages to save her  life showing a glimpse of the skill that  he later uses to save frodo from his  deadly knife wound  however calebrion is so traumatized by  the experience that she never fully  recovers her former health  and eventually decides to leave  middle-earth to seek peace and healing  in the blessed  realm the same place that frodo sails to  at the end of the return of the king  after this experience arwen's brothers  become especially bitter towards the  orcs  and spend much of their time out hunting  their foes often teaming up with the  rangers in the process  eventually this morbid hobby even leads  to their being present when aragorn's  father is killed by orcs making his  young son chieftain in the process  and that serves as the perfect segue to  finally introduce arwin's bow  soon after one of arwin's trips back  from rivendale she decides to take a  walk and who should appear on the path  in front of her but a young man  tell everyone who the young man is  legolas this is no mere ranger  he is aragorn  at this point he's a 20 year old  stripling who's over the moon because he  just found out that he's the chieftain  of the dunadine and the heir to the  throne of gondor  when the two meet it's love at first  sight at least for aragorn  arwen is little more than amused by his  head over heels attraction to her beauty  nevertheless aragorn sets his heart on  pursuing the elvish maiden which quickly  leads to a gentle but stern  confrontation with elrod  the loremaster sadly prophesies to the  young ranger that arwin will only marry  him if he becomes king  and that in either case either she or  aragorn will eventually be heartbroken  by the situation  if sauron is defeated and aragorn made  king and all that you hope for comes  true you will still have to taste the  bitterness of mortality  the budding relationship between arwen  and aragorn brings up an interesting  question  are the two related the short answer is  well yes  in fact in return of the king when we  learn how they met and aragorn explains  who he is  arwen's immediate response is that we  are kin from afar  however while their bloodlines are  related it's definitely a very  very distant connection elrond's brother  is a man  and we mean literally a man named elros  with their unique family history the  brothers and their descendants are  allowed to choose between either an  immortal or a mortal life  while elrond chooses immortality his  brother opts for mortality  and ends up leading a nation of men  called numenor over time  that nation becomes one of sauron's  chief enemies and eventually  it's the numenoreans who set up the two  kingdoms of arnor and gondor  when arnor collapses its royal bloodline  secretly survives through the rangers  and their line of chieftains  the last of whom is aragorn in other  words  there are dozens of generations between  irwin and aragorn making them  technically but extremely distantly  related  considering the fact that tolkien came  from a world where english royalty  was constantly intermarrying with first  and second cousins  the magnitude of the distance in aragorn  and irwin's relationship would hardly  have seemed to be much of a concern  over time aragorn makes his case darwin  in a convincing yet  indirect way when he discovers that he  doesn't have a shot with elrond's  daughter at present the ranger leaves  rivendell and heads out into the wide  world to forge his own path  in the ensuing decades the exiled king  proves himself over and over again  he travels in the wild fights in  disguise in rohan and gondor  befriends gandalf and even makes a solo  trip far into the mysterious eastern and  southern regions of the continent  over this time the son of aerothorn  evolves from an inexperienced  young man to a battle-hardened warrior  he gains wisdom and experience beyond  his years  [Music]  years that quickly add up while still a  drop in the bucket  the next time he meets arwen aragorn is  around 50 years old  this time they hook up in lothlorien and  it's arwin who's surprised to see  aragorn walking towards her through the  trees  they spend a season together and darwin  officially falls in love with the now  mature  exiled king by the time they part ways  they've committed to marrying each other  and elrond gives his sad approval  though he clarifies that his daughter  will marry no one less than the king of  arnor and gondor  after she's officially betrothed to  aragorn arwen spends most of the next  few decades in rivendale as things ramp  up to the war of the ring  once the war breaks out her role varies  depending on if you're reading the books  or watching the movies  arwen remains more or less on the  sidelines throughout the book series  however just because she's out of sight  doesn't mean she's out of mind  in fact aragorn is depicted thinking of  her on multiple occasions  for instance when the fellowship arrives  in la florian frodo sees the ranger  wrapped in a memory in the same place  where he and arwen got engaged  in addition to remembrances arwen meets  with aragorn when he arrives with the  hobbits in rivendell  she also personally makes an enormous  banner that aragorn unveils during the  battle of pelinor fields  using it to officially announce his  return as the king  arwen's role was dramatically beefed up  for peter jackson's trilogy  the shielf helps frodo escape from the  black riders revives a waterlogged  aragorn via a vision  and is repeatedly shown struggling with  her father's desire for her to leave  middle-earth  rather than become mortal even in this  enhanced role though  harwin's participation in the plot is  actually reduced from the original  cinematic scripts  in an interview with ign liv tyler  herself clarified that the character was  supposed to be much more heavily  involved in the story  in an earlier version of the script she  was actually supposed to lead the elf  contingent that fights it helms deep in  the two towers  however as they shot the movie the  filmmakers realized that inserting arwen  into the violent action  wasn't doing justice to her character  tyler herself points out that  you don't have to put a sword in a  woman's hand to make her seem tough  that's little actual makeup of who arwin  is and her sheer will and strength and  love for this man was strong enough  whether she had been depicted in battle  or not though there's no doubt that  arwen is more present in the film  adaptations than she ever is in the  original the lord of the rings story  both the written and cinematic versions  of arwin's story reconnect when she  marries aragorn  in all of the annals of middle-earth  lore this becomes just the third time  that an elf and a man get hitched  with both of the previous unions also  taking place in their shared family tree  their nuptials are a high water mark in  the story of the two lovers  of course arwen's marriage also means  she officially becomes the queen of the  reunited realms of gondor and arnor  practically overnight this makes her the  most powerful woman in all of middle  earth  as queen she helps aragorn rule their  kingdom for 120 years  during that time she brings sam's  daughter eleanor to be her maid of honor  in minas tirith  she also has at least two daughters who  remain nameless  and a son named eldarian who eventually  succeeds his father to the throne  one of the most complicated and  confusing elements about arwen's life  is her mortality or more accurately her  immortality that is replaced by  mortality  in essence arwen is allowed to choose  between being immortal or mortal  since she has both elves and men in her  ancestry the life of the elders leaving  you  this was my choice however  the specific ground rules for this  choice are never clarified by tolkien  it would appear that at least once  someone chooses to be mortal  the choice isn't given to their children  regardless of the specifics  arwin lives as an immortal elf until she  gives up her immortality to marry  aragorn  i choose a mortal life  this enables her to grant frodo her spot  on the ship headed west to the blessed  realm  but it also sets her up for a painfully  difficult parting as well  when arwyn discusses her choice to  become mortal in the return of the king  she explains that she is chosen quote  both the sweet and the bitter  and 120 years after her marriage to  aragorn the bitter part really comes  home to roost  after he passes and aldarian becomes  king it says in the book that in her  sorrow  she says goodbye to her children and her  loved ones and then she leaves minas  tirith returning to her past home of  lothlorien  however by this time galadriel and her  people have left  and no elves remain in the golden wood  she wanders there through the following  winter and then finally lies down to  rest in the same place where she had  betrothed herself to aragorn so many  years before  there tasting the bitterness at the very  end of her mortal life she passes away  quietly  eventually surrounded by a green grave  that will never change until the world  itself is altered  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite fantasy characters are  coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one
 

